WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.290
- Once I remove the battery,

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.150
I can safely disconnect the cables,

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.790
and we can get started on
the battery replacement.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.520
- Every iPhone battery is going to expire.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.950
That is, it's going to go bad over time

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.350
for some pretty technical reasons.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:14.850
So a few months ago,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.310
I shared that my iPhone 14 Pro's battery

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.640
had depleted faster than
my previous iPhones.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.830
- Joanna Stern actually mentioned

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.170
that hers is down to 88% capacity.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.670
- [Reporter] Wall Street
Journalist Joanna Stern

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.850
said her iPhone 14 Pro is down to 88%.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.270
- And I shared some hidden
numbers about its battery health.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.590
As of today, as the battery
hits 494 charge cycles,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.440
the battery health is at 87%.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.420
But what the heck do those numbers mean?

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.620
Where can you find them?

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.120
And why did this battery
deplete so fast in just a year?

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.883
I found the answers to
all of that and more.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.280
There's no such thing

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.860
as a lithium ion battery
that lasts forever.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.920
So Apple lets you know just
how healthy your battery is

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.300
in the software.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.080
Go to Settings, Battery,
Battery Health, and Charging.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.060
See that maximum capacity?

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.910
That's a measure of the
battery capacity relative

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.140
to when it was new.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.630
When I first unboxed this iPhone 14 Pro,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.310
it was at 100%.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.970
Now, a year later, it's at 87%.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.890
Yes, as that number gets lower,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.170
the battery doesn't hold a charge as long.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.390
Think of it like a shrinking gas tank,

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:25.470
and it's when that number gets

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.070
into the low 80% range
that you start to worry.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.750
According to Apple's website,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.140
a normal battery is
designed to retain up to 80%

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.370
of its original capacity at
500 complete charge cycles

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.800
when operating under normal conditions.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.059
But what's a charge cycle?

00:01:43.059 --> 00:01:46.230
(upbeat music)

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.730
Safety first.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.630
Obviously, I got a
bicycle to explain cycles.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:52.710
There we go.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.020
All right, 500 cycles.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.210
One, two.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.470
How's this working?

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.710
Okay, let's pretend this
wheel is your battery.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.110
You complete one charge
cycle when the battery goes

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.490
from 100% to 0%.

00:02:08.375 --> 00:02:10.163
It's basically like the Tour de France.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.250
But you don't complete a
cycle every time you charge.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.890
Say you use 80% of your battery one day,

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.920
and then recharge it to 100% overnight.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.350
If you use 20% the next day,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.620
you will have discharged a total of 100%.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.580
So yes, it could take multiple
days to complete one cycle.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.530
That lithium ion battery in your phone

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.070
is rated for 500 of these cycles.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.390
Anyone have a snack?

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.310
Nearing 400 cycles.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.143
Thank you.

00:02:40.634 --> 00:02:42.150
This is how they do it.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:42.983
450.

00:02:42.983 --> 00:02:45.330
And as you get closer to 500 cycles,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.420
the more that battery capacity
percentage is gonna go down.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.570
And yes, the battery will
drain faster when you use it.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.243
And 500.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.753
Why is it getting so slow?

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.423
Why is is it so hard to pedal?

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.480
How do you find out the
number of charge cycles

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.470
on your phone?

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:07.770
Well, the new iPhone 15
models actually show it.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.110
Go to Settings, General, About,

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.570
then scroll down to Cycle Count.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:13.590
For other iPhones,

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.170
you can download this
software called coconutBattery

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.070
to your Mac.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.220
When you plug in your phone,
it will show the cycle count,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.190
which brings me back to my iPhone 14 Pro.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:27.240
I had hit 494 charge cycles in a year,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.450
which makes me seem like
a total phone addict,

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.090
which, obviously, I am not,

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.440
but I don't think I use the phone

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.750
that much more than my iPhone 12 Pro,

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.680
which after two years of
use had 652 charging cycles

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.380
and 88% battery health.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.790
But I had two leading theories

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.280
about why the 14 expired quicker.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.230
Number one, software.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.230
Some app or some part of
iOS was causing the battery

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.520
to drain faster.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.780
Thus, I was charging it more,
so more cycle counts happened.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.550
It's gonna hurt tomorrow.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.500
Number two, heat.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.880
I did a previous video on this,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.760
but heat is the number
one enemy of batteries.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.203
- Oh no, it's smoking.
- Okay, yeah, it is.

00:04:07.203 --> 00:04:08.036
It is.

00:04:08.036 --> 00:04:10.080
So maybe something inside
my phone was getting warm

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.630
or causing the battery to fail quicker.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:13.950
When I took it to an Apple store,

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.410
I was told there was no hardware issue.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:17.850
An Apple spokesman pointed me

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.230
to the company's website,

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.330
and said that given the cycles,

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.690
my battery's performance was
in line with what's promised.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.150
Okay, so this battery expired quicker,

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.210
but it will happen to all
iPhone batteries eventually,

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.220
which brings us to two choices.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.790
Buy a new iPhone, or replace your battery,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.710
which is gonna be a lot cheaper

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.140
and better for the environment.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.870
So I went to a local New
York City repair shop

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.360
to explore the replacement options.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.820
How many battery replacements
would you say you do?

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:48.960
- Battery replacements are actually

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.640
one of our most common repairs,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.590
especially on Apple devices.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:56.340
I would say we get a
few devices every day,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.960
even though it's a common misconception

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.540
that the battery can't be
replaced because, you know,

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.790
back in the day, we used
to just pop the back open,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.770
replace the battery with
something we found online,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:05.603
and that was it.

00:05:05.603 --> 00:05:08.040
The batteries are actually
very easily replaceable,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.140
usually takes about 15 to 20 minutes

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.900
- [Joanna] But not all battery
replacements are equal.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.000
Joe here offers two options.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.800
- So the battery we have here on the left

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.010
would be the Apple original
versus an aftermarket battery,

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.170
which we purchase from suppliers

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.630
that produce them for shops like us.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.480
- [Joanna] Those aftermarket
or third-party batteries tend

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.760
to work as well according to Joe,

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.030
but you will get the following
message on the phone.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.790
- That basically tells you
that it's unable to determine

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.520
if your iPhone battery
is a genuine Apple part,

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.780
and it tells you to go to Settings

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.280
to get more information about it.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.110
- You can dismiss that initial popup,

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.540
but it will still show in Settings.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:46.710
Plus, you won't be able to see

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.020
all that battery health information.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.400
An Apple spokesman pointed me

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.860
to a report citing a study by the UL,

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.150
saying that in tests,

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.550
third-party batteries
overwhelmingly failed

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.800
to meet safety standards.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.280
Joe says he buys safe,
high-quality batteries.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.350
The easiest way to avoid the error message

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.780
is to get your battery
swapped at an Apple store,

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.060
or an Apple-authorized repair shop.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.250
And honestly, it might cost about the same

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.600
to do it there too.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.640
Joe said it would cost $89

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.150
for the iPhone 14 Pro battery
replacement at his store.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.583
The Apple store quotes $99.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.430
Why should I opt to come here?

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.680
- The Apple's warranty
is a 90-day warranty

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:26.730
on the batteries.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.290
We offer a six-month warranty.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.410
We also are able to replace
the battery within 10

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.400
to 20 minutes,

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.650
versus Apple's lengthy process,

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.000
sometimes can take an hour,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.580
sometimes two or three
depending on how busy they are.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.620
- So what did we learn here today?

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.590
Well, if you don't want
your battery to go bad,

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.300
don't charge your phone.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:46.200
Seriously though,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.410
you can keep those charge
cycles down by reducing use.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.230
Relying on low power mode will help too.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:54.840
There's also a new
setting on the iPhone 15

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.350
that will stop charging
at 80% to preserve health,

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.590
and definitely keep your
phone out of the heat,

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.980
but you can't live your
life babying your battery.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.130
It's like, I guess we'll
just be sitting here,

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:09.513
preservin' charge cycles.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:11.863
Great.

00:07:11.863 --> 00:07:14.002
(upbeat music)

00:07:14.002 --> 00:07:15.956
(Joanna sighs)

00:07:15.956 --> 00:07:18.706
(Joanna exhales)

